<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alba Iulia</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gyulafehérvár, Karlsburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alba Iulia, in Hungarian Gyulafehérvár, is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, on the Mureș River, built on the site of the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress of Apulum. It was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Transylvania from the 11th century and the capital of the Principality of Transylvania in the 16th and 17th centuries. On 1 December 1918 an assembly there proclaimed the union of Transylvania with Romania.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alba-iulia.jpg" alt="Alba Iulia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mihai Raducanu · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cetatea_Alba_Carolina_Ansamblul_fortifica%C8%9Biei_%E2%80%9ECetatea_Alba_Iulia_14.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Alba Iulia is the seat of Alba County in west-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, on the river Mureș in the historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. Its names reflect its layers of history. In Roman times the settlement was called Apulum, from the Dacian Apoulon mentioned by Ptolemy. From the 9th to the 11th centuries it bore the Slavic name Bălgrad, white castle. The Hungarian name Gyulafehérvár refers to Gyula II, a mid-10th-century Hungarian warlord baptised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> later called it Karlsburg, in honour of Charles VI.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Nearby stood the Dacian centre of Apulon. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a> became a Roman province the capital of Dacia Apulensis was established here, and Apulum, the largest urban centre in Roman Dacia, was the seat of the XIII Gemina Legion; its castrum, at 37.5 hectares, is the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesta-hungarorum">Gesta Hungarorum</a> a regent named Jula or Geula, grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-i-of-hungary">Stephen I of Hungary</a>, built his capital here in the 10th century and about 950 built the first church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>. After Stephen adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> a Catholic bishopric was established, and the present cathedral dates from the 12th or 13th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hunyadi">John Hunyadi</a> used the citadel in 1442 to prepare against the Ottoman Turks and was entombed in the cathedral.</p><p>After the partition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> the city became in 1542 the capital of the autonomous Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, until 1690. On 29 November 1599 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-the-brave">Michael the Brave</a> of Wallachia entered it and became Voivode of Transylvania, uniting Wallachia, Moldavia and Transylvania under his rule until he was murdered in 1601. Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-bethlen">Gábor Bethlen</a> founded an academy there. The city passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1690, and between 1716 and 1735 a seven-bastion baroque fortress, Alba Carolina, was built for Charles VI. On 1 December 1918 the National Assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> of Transylvania and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> gathered there to proclaim the union of Transylvania with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and in 1922 Ferdinand I was crowned king in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> cathedral.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Upper Town, with the fortress, contains the Roman Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> cathedrals, the Princely Palace, the Union Hall and the Batthyaneum Library, founded in 1780, which holds half of the 9th-century Codex Aureus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch">Lorsch</a>. The Orthodox cathedral was built between 1921 and 1923. The city is historically important for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, Hungarians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons">Transylvanian Saxons</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alba Iulia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Suleiman seized Buda in 1541, the court moved to <b>Gyulafehérvár</b>. The Diet of Torda in 1568 proclaimed broad religious freedom.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-hungarian-kingdom">Eastern Hungarian Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>By the Treaty of Speyer in 1570, John Sigismund Zápolya renounced the Hungarian royal title and became Prince of Transylvania, ruling Transylvania and the Partium, eastern Hungarian counties, under Ottoman suzerainty with his court at <b>Gyulafehérvár</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-transylvania">Principality of Transylvania</a></li><li><blockquote>Their seat was probably at <b>Gyulafehérvár</b> (Alba Iulia), on a Roman fort.</blockquote><a href="/state/realm-of-the-gyulas">Realm of the Gyulas</a></li></ul></section>
